# No splice after kill-calls (#invfast-fault3)

Words: [README.md — Words used](../../README.md#words-used-in-this-project).
Copy 1 (copy 2: [docs/GAME_DESIGN.md](../../docs/GAME_DESIGN.md) FAST rule 6).

INVFAST froze on the board — **only** on a hit, never a miss, 100%
repeatable — the instant a bullet actually killed an alien, hit the
saucer, or a power-up was collected. Fault 3, `obj=`/`arr=`/`envl=`
completely flat right up to the exact frame it happens. **Does not
reproduce in Chrome** — a machine-specific wall, not a JS bug.

Every broken site had the same shape: several calls (score, particle
effect, state flip), **then** `.splice()`, all synchronous, often
nested a call or two deep. The never-rewritten `INVADERS.HTML` avoided
this by accident — it deferred the whole kill into
`setTimeout(() => {...})`, which only ever runs after that frame's
loops are fully finished, never nested inside them. The FAST rewrite
correctly dropped `setTimeout` (a per-call allocation) but made the
splice synchronous instead, landing it mid-iteration over the arrays
it mutates. Whatever pool that pressures is a real limit (RTL side is
chasing the mechanism — leading theory: the per-call environment pool,
exhausted faster than sweep reclaims it); the old code just never
exercised it.

**Fix: mark dead now, sweep once later — same O(n) cost, not slower.**

```javascript
// BAD — calls then splice, synchronous, mid-iteration (board fault 3)
bumpScore(invader.points());
createParticles(invader, "#BAA0DE");
invaders.splice(i, 1);
projectiles.splice(j, 1);

// GOOD — mark now (free), sweep once after this loop is fully done
invader.dead = true;
bullet.dead = true;
// ...
function sweepInvaders(list) {
  var i, w;
  w = 0;
  for (i = 0; i < list.length; i++) {
    if (!list[i].dead) { list[w] = list[i]; w = w + 1; }
  }
  list.length = w;
}
```

`storage/INVFAST.HTML`'s `updateInvaderCollision` /
`sweepGridInvaders` / `sweepProjectiles` / `sweepInvaderProjectiles` /
`sweepPowerUps` are the reference pattern. A **bare** removal with no
score/effect call before it (offscreen cull, plain bounds check) never
froze — splice directly there. The wall is "several calls, then
remove," not array removal itself.
